 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-007\", \"clauses\": {\"compose\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"{question}\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 draws bank rows that put shapes together, such as the row whose question says two triangles are placed together and the row whose question says two identical squares are placed side by side. Those rows are keyed to a composed shape.\"}, \"decompose\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"{question}\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 draws bank rows that cut a shape into pieces, such as the row whose question says a square is cut on a slant from one corner to the opposite corner and the row whose question says a long rectangle is cut across its width into two equal squares.\"}, \"triangles\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"{question}\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 draws the row about two triangles placed together (answer keyed as rectangle) and the row about a square cut into two triangles (answer keyed as two triangles), so triangles are produced as the object of compose and decompose.\"}, \"squares\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"{question}\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 draws the row about two identical squares placed side by side, the row about a square cut on a slant, and the row about a rectangle cut into two equal squares, so squares are produced as the object.\"}, \"rectangles\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"{question}\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 draws the row about two identical squares forming a rectangle and the row about a rectangle cut down the middle into two equal pieces, so rectangles are produced as the object.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F08\", \"F09\", \"F10\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"{answer}\", \"{distractors}\", \"{question}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Three rows of the _ITEM_POOL bank, each printed whole through F10 with its keyed answer (F08) and distractors (F09), are wrong or misleading. (1) The row whose question begins Two identical triangles are joined along their matching straight edges has a first sentence of 18 words, above the item's sentence_max_words of 15, which a grade 1 learner cannot be expected to read. Its stem also says only a 4-sided shape with equal sides, which fits a rhombus as well as a square, so the keyed answer square (F08) is not forced; the distractor rectangle (F09) is not excluded either. (2) The row whose question begins Two triangles are placed together has keyed answer rectangle, which holds only if the triangles are right triangles; the stem does not say so, and for right isosceles triangles the distractor square (F09) is also correct. (3) The row whose question begins A rectangle is cut into two equal pieces down the middle has distractor two squares, which is correct for a rectangle twice as long as it is wide, and the row whose question begins A long rectangle is cut across its width has distractor two rectangles, which is also true because a square is a rectangle. Under Rule 1 these keys are not safe for every value the domains allow. The hint families F01 to F04 and the option-letter families F05 to F07 contain no defect the packet shows.\"}",
